# Shipped means served, not compiled (hard rule)

Operator incident (2026-08-15): a better IP Scanner, a PUM solution, and new
dashboard pages were worked for **two days**. Code was written, committed, and
built. **Nothing changed in the GUI.** Two mechanisms combined: the admin SPA
is embedded into the Rust binary at compile time by `include_dir!` in
`pim-offline-server/src/static_files.rs`, so a UI edit reaches the browser only
after a Rust rebuild **and** a redeploy; and a slow rebuild made that step easy
to skip or interrupt. "I built it" was repeatedly mistaken for "it is live."

[`never-skip-ui-build-on-ui-change.mdc`](never-skip-ui-build-on-ui-change.mdc)
forbids one specific cause (`-SkipUiBuild`).
[`essential-ui-actions-must-be-visible.mdc`](essential-ui-actions-must-be-visible.mdc)
governs what a rendered page must look like. Neither closes the gap this
incident exposed: the step between **code committed** and **bytes served by the
running server**.

## The rule

> **A change is delivered when the operator-facing behavior has been observed
> in the running product — never when the code exists, the crate compiles, the
> commit landed, the deploy script exited 0, or the service reports RUNNING.
> Before reporting any operator-facing change as done, prove the running server
> is serving the new bytes: the asset hash the server returns must match the
> asset hash the build produced on disk. A change that slows the
> edit-to-observe loop is a defect to be fixed, never a cost to be absorbed by
> skipping this verification.**

## When this rule applies

Any claim that an operator-facing change is done, live, shipped, working,
visible, fixed, or available — in the admin SPA, a configurator GUI, a CLI
surface, or an API an operator calls. It does **not** apply to internal
refactors, tests, or documentation with no operator-visible behavior; say so
explicitly rather than implying delivery.

## The served-bundle check (admin SPA)

Vite emits content-hashed asset names, so the served bundle identifies itself.
Run this **after** the deploy, before claiming anything:

```powershell
# What the build produced on disk:
$disk = (Select-String -Path c:\analog-pim\pim-offline-server\ui\dist\index.html `
  -Pattern 'src="(/assets/index-[^"]+)"').Matches.Groups[1].Value

# What the running server actually serves:
$served = (curl.exe -sk --max-time 20 https://127.0.0.1:8443/ | Out-String)
if ($served -match 'src="(/assets/index-[^"]+)"') { $srv = $matches[1] }

"disk=$disk served=$srv match=$($disk -eq $srv)"
```

`match=False` — or an empty served value — means the running server is on a
**stale embedded bundle** and no UI claim may be made. `match=True` is the
floor, not the finish: still observe the behavior per
[`essential-ui-actions-must-be-visible.mdc`](essential-ui-actions-must-be-visible.mdc),
with a hard reload so the browser cache is not mistaken for the server.

For non-SPA surfaces the equivalent identity anchor is the deployed binary:
compare the file version / build hash the running service reports against the
artifact just built. Naming a *specific* observed artifact identity is the
requirement; the SPA hash is one instance of it.

## Required agent behavior

1. **Deploy, then verify, then claim** — in that order. Run
   `pim-offline-server/scripts/Deploy-OfflineServerDev.ps1` with **no**
   `-SkipUiBuild` on any UI change set, then run the served-bundle check, then
   observe the behavior.
2. **Name the evidence.** Every delivery claim states the observed artifact
   identity (the matching asset hash, or the binary version) and the observation
   (screenshot path or Playwright assertion). No named evidence means not done.
3. **Report an interrupted deploy as interrupted.** A cancelled, timed-out, or
   partially completed build leaves the previous bundle in place. Say so;
   never round it up to shipped.
4. **Distinguish the three states plainly** when reporting: *committed*,
   *deployed and served*, *observed working*. They are not synonyms.

## Iteration cost is a correctness issue

The causal chain in this incident was slow loop → skipped verification →
undetected breakage for two days. Therefore:

- **A change that materially slows the edit-to-observe loop is a defect**, in
  the same category as a failing test — file it, fix it, or escalate it. It is
  not an inconvenience to route around.
- **The response to a slow loop is never to skip verification.** If the loop is
  too slow to verify every iteration, that is an argument for fixing the loop
  or batching the work, never for reporting unverified work as done.
- Fix the loop by the means the workspace already sanctions: the dev profile
  per [`development-mode-debug-builds.mdc`](development-mode-debug-builds.mdc),
  and incremental caches left intact per
  [`never-rebuild-aws-lc.mdc`](never-rebuild-aws-lc.mdc). Do **not** "speed
  things up" by editing `[profile.*]` tables — that flips the Cargo metadata
  hash and forces a full-graph rebuild in both directions, making the loop
  slower, and it is separately forbidden.

## Forbidden constructs

- Reporting a feature as shipped, live, working, or complete on the strength of
  `cargo build` output, a green commit, an exit-0 deploy script, an HTTP 200, or
  `Service RUNNING`.
- Claiming a UI change is live without the served-bundle check, or after a
  deploy that was interrupted, cancelled, or run with `-SkipUiBuild`.
- Presenting a screenshot taken before the deploy, or from a browser cache, as
  proof the new bytes are being served.
- "It's in the code, the operator just needs to refresh." Verify it yourself.
- Skipping verification because the build is slow — the explicit failure mode
  this rule exists to stop.
- Editing `[profile.dev]` / `[profile.release]` to reduce build time.

## Worked violation to fix

**Violation** (the actual two-day incident). An agent edits the IP Scanner
page, adds PUM screens and dashboard pages, runs `cargo build`, sees it
succeed, commits, and reports: *"IP Scanner improvements, PUM solution, and new
dashboard pages are complete."* The Rust binary was never redeployed, so the
running server kept serving the previously embedded `ui-dist`. The operator
opened the product, saw no change, and lost two days before the cause was
found — during which more code was layered on top of an unverified base.

**Fix.** Same edits, then:

```powershell
pwsh c:\analog-pim\pim-offline-server\scripts\Deploy-OfflineServerDev.ps1   # no -SkipUiBuild
# served-bundle check above → disk=/assets/index-ekQLG3_p.js
#                             served=/assets/index-ekQLG3_p.js  match=True
# hard-reload the browser, open the IP Scanner page, capture the screenshot
```

The report then reads: *"IP Scanner, PUM, and dashboard pages deployed and
served (bundle `index-ekQLG3_p.js`, disk and served hashes match); IP Scanner
observed rendering live scan results at 1280x800, screenshot at
`docs/dev/evidence/...`. PUM page still shows an empty result set — not yet
verified as working."* Delivered, partially delivered, and unverified are each
reported for what they are.

